@Pavlo — normative call: non-normative, move it. The Namespaces section is already (LOCKED: no on-chain registry; out-of-band convention) — agent-attestations, agent-attestations:wyriwe, and the ERC-8004 reference were never doing normative work there, just naming an example consumer. Relocating them to Reference Implementation keeps the base transport-only/stack-agnostic, which is the stated intent of that section. No coupling lost, agreed with your read.

That's my (8309/8299) side settled — Damon still needs to confirm since he's co-author too.

Separately: this is one of five edits we owe Obaresearch on #1826 (promised 24 Sept, none landed yet — cursor/since ordering and the future-dated-bound tolerance are the two substantive ones, this and the ENS-rooting/peer-abuse SHOULDs are a sentence each). Good that cursor/since checks clean against erc-8309-vantage's schema and golden vectors with zero overlap. Will get all five into one PR edit and post the diff in the Magicians thread once it lands, per the commitment there — not before.

Agreed with Pavlo that Settlement -> Reputation is the boundary to pin first. A concrete starting point for verifyOutcome(orderId, proof), for the authors to confirm or correct before anything is built:

What the proof must let any verifier recompute from the chain, never from the facilitator's report:
- chainId, plus the settlement tx hash and log index of the escrow's release (or settlement) event for this exact orderId;
- the asset Transfer to the provider in that same tx receipt, with the amount and payee checked against the order;
- a successful receipt status.
Logs come from that tx's receipt only, not from a scan of the block. That's the x402#3824 lesson Petchevere pointed at.

Vectors, in the attester-diversity-v0 style:
- positive: a release for orderId, amount and payee match -> accepted;
- facilitator-signed report only, no tx -> rejected;
- a valid tx for a DIFFERENT orderId -> rejected;
- amount or payee mismatch -> rejected;
- reverted tx -> rejected;
- the right Transfer in the same block but a different tx -> rejected.
Each comes with a mutation control showing a lax verifier passes it.

@OnThe_Stars, does that match what verifyOutcome should require under 8275? If it does, we'll build the vectors and a stdlib checker in the reputation conformance folder, credited to Petchevere for the gap and Pavlo for the boundary framing, and open them as a PR for review. Nothing is pinned until you say the semantics are right.

@Echo no collision on our side. /prove's leg-2 reveal is a pure check of sha256(JCS(content)) against the stored commitment_hash, so content.blinding is one more committed field, and content_bound / content_commitment_mismatch work unchanged. +1 on MUST always.

On numbers: when we pinned leg 2 in July we limited content to strings, hex and integers for the same float reason Pavlo raised, so integer coordinates (1e-7 degrees, cm) fit what is already pinned.

One addition for the vector set: a blinding that is present but not 64 lowercase hex should be refused at validation, not just fail the commitment, so a malformed salt never looks like a tampered reveal.

